WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.740
Today I'd like to revisit some really big numbers we've covered recently on Numberphile

00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:08.478
and then maybe reflect on how big they really are.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:14.160
Now, it all starts with this video we made about the sum of three cubes.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.320
[Past video] And this is a sort of area of

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:23.199
research that I'm, I'm actively engaged with.
- Tim Browning told us how all numbers could be expressed as the sum of three cubes.

00:00:23.279 --> 00:00:28.879
For example, ten can be represented as two cubed, plus one cubed,

00:00:29.439 --> 00:00:32.879
plus one cubed. There you go, the sum of three cubed numbers.

00:00:33.739 --> 00:00:35.759
Eleven can be three

00:00:36.719 --> 00:00:38.239
minus two

00:00:38.239 --> 00:00:41.839
and minus two. That's right, you can use negative numbers as well.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.700
Seventeen is as simple as 2,

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:51.439
2, and 1; while 45 can be 4, minus 3, and 2.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:59.759
Now look I've already told a little fib here, because not all integers can be expressed as the sum of three cubes.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:05.599
In fact, it's been proven that numbers of these forms can't be, meaning these integers will never work.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:11.839
You'll never get them as the sum of three cubes. Sorry about that. But all these other ones should have a solution.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.400
I *say* should but here's a problem, at the time we made the video

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:22.879
there were three numbers below 100 that should have been possible, that hadn't been found.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.939
33, 42 and 74.

00:01:27.119 --> 00:01:34.319
A Numberphile viewer, Sander Huisman, let a powerful computer loose on the problem and he cracked 74.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:37.519
Look at this - three absolute telephone numbers.

00:01:38.159 --> 00:01:41.118
But that still left 33 and 42.

00:01:42.079 --> 00:01:49.139
Andrew Booker from the University of Bristol refined the algorithms and used this supercomputer to solve 33.

00:01:50.079 --> 00:01:52.079
Here's what he found,

00:01:52.479 --> 00:01:57.999
some real whoppers again. That left 42, but with some extra help - you can guess what happened?

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.380
Here it is.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:03.680
42 cracked as well. Still no rigorous proof

00:02:03.759 --> 00:02:07.839
that all numbers are possible, that glorious moment still awaits mathematics.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:14.959
But at least those three glaring elephants in the room below 100 were removed. But here's something else I didn't tell you,

00:02:15.599 --> 00:02:23.359
not only do mathematicians think all integers are possible; they think they're possible in an infinite number of ways.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:27.759
Take 10 for example. I've already showed you 2, 1 and 1,

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.800
but there's also 4, -3, and -3.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:39.839
And these ones as well, and as I said, in all likelihood the options continue forever.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:45.039
So this solution for 42, that just happens to be the first one among the number line.

00:02:45.519 --> 00:02:49.039
Let the computers search for long enough and they'll find another, and

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:53.039
another, and another. And that's going to bring us to number 3 - because you see,

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:59.759
the number 3 is a bit of a pin-up in this area of research. Since mathematicians first started dealing with this problem

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.500
it's been used to exemplify the whole conundrum.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:06.080
Now I'm sure you can think of this one very obvious solution,

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:11.679
1, 1 and 1. And the next solution isn't too tricky either - minus five,

00:03:12.239 --> 00:03:14.239
four, and four.

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:18.799
But if this whole infinite solution thing is true, where's the next one?

00:03:19.519 --> 00:03:26.419
It was so hard to find, this famous paper on the topic noted "it was very difficult indeed", classic understatement.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:31.839
So Andrew here decided the third solution for 3, that was his new holy grail.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.400
Again, they unleashed the computers and you can guess what's coming - here it is everyone:

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:44.239
The next solution for 3. Now, let's just focus on that first number, the positive one. It's got 21 digits.

